00:38:20.892 --> 00:38:24.936
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:38:24.936 --> 00:38:31.364
Well I would have to say, that, you need
both.

00:38:31.364 --> 00:38:33.870
You need emotion and you need logic.

00:38:33.870 --> 00:38:37.176
So, in the beginning, I was purely
emotional.

00:38:37.176 --> 00:38:38.270
Made a lot of mistakes.

00:38:39.662 --> 00:38:41.114
I happened to be sitting.

00:38:41.114 --> 00:38:47.230
I was sharing this story with Dean Saloner
just before he came on.

00:38:49.420 --> 00:38:50.820
I was sitting in Nelson

00:38:53.290 --> 00:38:54.620
Mandela's living room.

00:38:54.620 --> 00:38:58.055
And I'm not just saying that to name drop.

00:38:58.055 --> 00:38:58.240
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:38:58.240 --> 00:39:01.622
&gt;&gt; I was actually sitting there.

00:39:01.622 --> 00:39:02.604
&gt;&gt; You stayed with him, right.

00:39:02.604 --> 00:39:06.880
&gt;&gt; I stayed with him for, stayed with him
for ten days.

00:39:06.880 --> 00:39:08.705
And as I said to the dean [UNKNOWN] I
could

00:39:08.705 --> 00:39:11.799
have, I literally could have written a
book called 29 meals.

00:39:11.799 --> 00:39:14.983
Cuz I had 29 meals with him at that
particular time.

00:39:14.983 --> 00:39:15.925
I wish I had.

00:39:15.925 --> 00:39:16.688
&gt;&gt; Yeah you should.

00:39:16.688 --> 00:39:17.337
&gt;&gt; I should've.

00:39:17.337 --> 00:39:18.877
&gt;&gt; You should do it now.

00:39:18.877 --> 00:39:20.632
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:39:20.632 --> 00:39:22.185
&gt;&gt; I didn't record it so some things I

00:39:22.185 --> 00:39:24.335
think was that the 2nd meal or the 12th
meal.

00:39:24.335 --> 00:39:29.480
Anyway, so I was sitting in Nelson,
sitting with Adiva and.

00:39:29.480 --> 00:39:35.810
We were talking about how, how do you
really make an impact in the world.

00:39:35.810 --> 00:39:40.265
And we were reading the paper and we, I'd
reached the point where

00:39:40.265 --> 00:39:43.730
I was no longer like, oh my God what am I
gonna say.

00:39:43.730 --> 00:39:47.000
Cuz we were just sitting in silence
reading the paper.

00:39:48.380 --> 00:39:53.020
And there was an article in the paper
about, you know, some tragic situation.

00:39:53.020 --> 00:39:57.720
And we both started talking about the way
to end poverty is through education.

00:39:59.040 --> 00:40:03.230
And I said to him, I really at some point
would like to build a school over there.

00:40:03.230 --> 00:40:05.516
And then, he got up and called the
minister to education,

00:40:05.516 --> 00:40:07.812
and said get over here now, Oprah wants to
build a school.

00:40:07.812 --> 00:40:11.440
[LAUGH].

00:40:11.440 --> 00:40:13.952
And I was like well I was thinking about
it.

00:40:13.952 --> 00:40:16.922
[LAUGH].

00:40:16.922 --> 00:40:19.688
I didn't say I wanted to do it today.

00:40:19.688 --> 00:40:20.729
[LAUGH].

00:40:20.729 --> 00:40:24.706
But so we literally started the process
then.

00:40:24.706 --> 00:40:28.736
It was an emotional decision for me in
that I think philanthropy

00:40:28.736 --> 00:40:32.927
should come out of you, your doing should
come out of your being.

00:40:32.927 --> 00:40:37.981
Everybody knows my story as a poor negro
child growing up in apartheid Mississippi.

00:40:37.981 --> 00:40:44.770
And if it were not for education and being
born at the right time.

00:40:44.770 --> 00:40:47.690
Cuz I was literally born in the year of
desegregation.

00:40:49.090 --> 00:40:53.220
Five years before, three years before, two
years before, nobody would of

00:40:53.220 --> 00:40:57.180
even had the hope that my life could of
been any different.

00:40:57.180 --> 00:40:59.450
So because I was born at that time, and
literally moved out of

00:41:01.460 --> 00:41:04.240
Mississippi by the time I was in my first
classroom.

00:41:04.240 --> 00:41:05.900
I was in Kindergarten.

00:41:05.900 --> 00:41:08.355
Wrote my kindergarten teacher a letter,
Ms. New.

00:41:08.355 --> 00:41:18.110
I said, dear Ms. New, I do not belong
here.

00:41:18.110 --> 00:41:18.180
&gt;&gt; Oh.

00:41:18.180 --> 00:41:21.630
&gt;&gt; Cuz I know a lot of big words.

00:41:21.630 --> 00:41:23.660
And then, I wrote every big word I knew.

00:41:23.660 --> 00:41:27.120
Elephant [LAUGH], hippopotamus,
Mississippi, Nicodemus.

00:41:27.120 --> 00:41:29.750
Shadrackmeshackinthebindigo from the
bible, so, and

00:41:29.750 --> 00:41:32.540
then Ms News says, who did this?

00:41:32.540 --> 00:41:33.012
I said, I did.

00:41:33.012 --> 00:41:42.050
So,they marched me off to the principals
office, the only time I was ever in there.

00:41:42.050 --> 00:41:42.550
Principals

00:41:44.650 --> 00:41:46.980
office, principal made me write those
words again and

00:41:46.980 --> 00:41:48.490
I got myself out of kindergarten, into
first grade.

00:41:48.490 --> 00:41:48.700
&gt;&gt; Oh my god.

00:41:48.700 --> 00:41:49.700
&gt;&gt; First grade, skipped second grade,
hellerher.

00:41:49.700 --> 00:41:49.920
[LAUGH].

00:41:49.920 --> 00:41:50.520
The Renaissance began.

00:41:50.520 --> 00:41:52.583
&gt;&gt; Yea.

00:41:52.583 --> 00:41:57.479
[LAUGH].

00:41:57.479 --> 00:42:01.838
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:42:01.838 --> 00:42:03.130
Yea, yea.

00:42:03.130 --> 00:42:05.158
&gt;&gt; You've always had this conviction.

00:42:05.158 --> 00:42:09.123
You've always, it seems like you've always
known who you are, even if you were.

00:42:09.123 --> 00:42:10.250
&gt;&gt; Well I knew I didn't belong there with
those kids.

00:42:10.250 --> 00:42:11.294
&gt;&gt; You knew that.

00:42:11.294 --> 00:42:12.601
&gt;&gt; In kindergarten, you're sitting there,
that's

00:42:12.601 --> 00:42:13.888
what I'm talking about listen to your
instincts.

00:42:13.888 --> 00:42:19.154
You're looking around and say these kids
[LAUGH]

00:42:19.154 --> 00:42:24.295
they are playing with some blocks
[CROSSTALK].

00:42:24.295 --> 00:42:27.648
And I know Nicodemus [LAUGH].

00:42:27.648 --> 00:42:33.069
I do not think I belong in here, I do not
belong in here so my point is,

00:42:33.069 --> 00:42:38.318
[LAUGH] my point is education really
opened the door as we all know.

00:42:38.318 --> 00:42:40.362
I'm not gonna give you the education
speech.

00:42:40.362 --> 00:42:42.093
How do you change a person's life.

00:42:42.093 --> 00:42:46.214
I had prior to starting my school in South
Africa,

00:42:46.214 --> 00:42:50.076
I had this big idea that I was going to,
emotional.

00:42:50.076 --> 00:42:55.844
That I was gonna take all, 100 families
out of the projects, in Cabrini Green.

00:42:55.844 --> 00:42:58.681
And I was gonna give them a new life and I

00:42:58.681 --> 00:43:02.865
was gonna buy them homes and stuff and
that did not work.

00:43:02.865 --> 00:43:04.532
It failed miserably.

00:43:04.532 --> 00:43:07.936
I had a Big Sister program that I started,
failed miserably.

00:43:07.936 --> 00:43:10.653
So I realized that for me.

00:43:10.653 --> 00:43:12.734
First of all, I realize you don't change,

00:43:12.734 --> 00:43:15.273
as you all are recognizing through the
seed program.

00:43:15.273 --> 00:43:21.178
You first have to change the way a person
thinks and see themselves.

00:43:21.178 --> 00:43:26.128
So you've gotta to create a sense of
aspiration, a sense of hopefulness so

00:43:26.128 --> 00:43:30.930
a person can see, can begin to even have a
vision for a better life.

00:43:30.930 --> 00:43:35.330
And if you can't connect to that, then,
then, then, then you, then you lose.

00:43:35.330 --> 00:43:36.390
You lose and they lose.

00:43:36.390 --> 00:43:38.610
And it's just money after money after
money.

00:43:38.610 --> 00:43:44.976
So, for me it's using my philanthropy to
do what I have found to be

00:43:44.976 --> 00:43:51.930
enormously, helpful.

00:43:51.930 --> 00:43:53.860
You know, the light in my life was
education.

00:43:53.860 --> 00:43:56.640
So for me, in the beginning when I started
to make

00:43:56.640 --> 00:44:00.800
money, especially when it's published,
everybody and your brother calls you.

00:44:00.800 --> 00:44:02.860
And then you've got to make a decision.

00:44:02.860 --> 00:44:05.470
Am I going to do what everybody else wants
me to do?

00:44:05.470 --> 00:44:08.893
Or, am I going to be led by who I really
am?

00:44:08.893 --> 00:44:11.402
And I learned, as will happen to anybody
who's successful in

00:44:11.402 --> 00:44:14.310
your family, people start treating you
like the First National Bank.

00:44:16.390 --> 00:44:18.744
And, you've got to decide.

00:44:18.744 --> 00:44:22.480
You've got to draw the boundaries for
yourself.

00:44:22.480 --> 00:44:27.920
And decide, how are you gonna use, your
money, your talent, your

00:44:27.920 --> 00:44:32.416
time, in such a way that it's going to
serve you first.

00:44:32.416 --> 00:44:36.458
Because if you, if it doesn't allow you to
be filled up.

00:44:36.458 --> 00:44:40.780
Then you get depleted and you no longer,
you can't keep doing it.

00:44:40.780 --> 00:44:44.115
So my decisions are now emotional and
logical.

00:44:44.115 --> 00:44:47.219
Meaning I choose education, but I do it in
such a

00:44:47.219 --> 00:44:51.736
way that's actually going to benefit the
person that I'm serving.

00:44:51.736 --> 00:44:53.791
Then it's not just, oh I want to help
people.

00:44:53.791 --> 00:44:55.996
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:44:55.996 --> 00:45:02.363
So to move on to our last part, you said
at the end of your 25 years, gratitude

00:45:02.363 --> 00:45:08.900
is the single greatest treasure I will
take with me from this experience.

00:45:08.900 --> 00:45:11.172
So now, you started your own network and
you continue

00:45:11.172 --> 00:45:13.659
to be very involved in your philanthropy
and your school.

00:45:15.750 --> 00:45:16.250
Is

00:45:21.760 --> 00:45:23.148
there anything left that you're scared to
try?

00:45:23.148 --> 00:45:24.936
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:45:24.936 --> 00:45:26.930
&gt;&gt; Whoa, Amanda.

00:45:26.930 --> 00:45:29.490
You must have been up all night long.

00:45:29.490 --> 00:45:30.180
&gt;&gt; I've prepared a little bit.

00:45:31.410 --> 00:45:31.780
Just a little.

00:45:31.780 --> 00:45:32.290
&gt;&gt; Oh, my goodness.

00:45:32.290 --> 00:45:33.380
Anything left that I'm scared to try?

00:45:37.931 --> 00:45:38.547
no.

00:45:38.547 --> 00:45:41.814
[LAUGH] No, and I'm just trying to think
what, I'm just

00:45:41.814 --> 00:45:46.070
trying to think, well, is there something
that I haven't thought of.

00:45:46.070 --> 00:45:49.330
&gt;&gt; Well, there's not much you haven't done
so.

00:45:49.330 --> 00:45:51.070
&gt;&gt; Well, but I stay in my lane.

00:45:51.070 --> 00:45:51.190
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:45:51.190 --> 00:45:53.310
&gt;&gt; I stay, I know where my lane is.

00:45:54.580 --> 00:45:55.780
I know what my lane is.

00:45:55.780 --> 00:45:59.520
I know that my real calling is what I said
earlier.

00:45:59.520 --> 00:46:02.110
I know what it looks like to the rest of
the world.

00:46:02.110 --> 00:46:05.458
Oh, she's a talk show celebrity but I
really know what I'm here

00:46:05.458 --> 00:46:08.310
to do, which is the number one thing I
would say to you.

00:46:08.310 --> 00:46:10.760
First let me answer your question.

00:46:10.760 --> 00:46:14.390
So no, there's nothing, I'm not scared to
try?

00:46:14.390 --> 00:46:20.110
I haven't even, I had hit my stride but I
haven't done what I ultimately came to do.

00:46:20.110 --> 00:46:22.980
There still is a supreme moment of destiny
that awaits

00:46:22.980 --> 00:46:25.420
me and I also knew that during the Oprah
Show.

00:46:25.420 --> 00:46:28.650
I've kept a journal since I was 15 years
old.

00:46:28.650 --> 00:46:29.901
It's so pitiful when you go back and

00:46:29.901 --> 00:46:31.715
see how pathetic you were as a person,
sometimes.

00:46:31.715 --> 00:46:32.830
[LAUGH].

00:46:32.830 --> 00:46:36.394
But I always knew even during that show,
that the show, we live

00:46:36.394 --> 00:46:40.052
in a fame culture, we lived in a fame
centered world, you know.

00:46:40.052 --> 00:46:41.902
Had this literally been during the

00:46:41.902 --> 00:46:45.490
Renaissance, people would have valued
different things.

00:46:45.490 --> 00:46:50.295
We've been doing the Transcendentalist
period, people valued

00:46:50.295 --> 00:46:54.230
different things but in our culture we
value fame.

00:46:54.230 --> 00:46:57.090
So I always understood that that was the

00:46:57.090 --> 00:47:00.468
basis for me being known, in the world
because

00:47:00.468 --> 00:47:03.762
people wouldn't be able to hear you,
unless

00:47:03.762 --> 00:47:07.620
you came with some swag or swagger, you
know?

00:47:07.620 --> 00:47:15.256
And I also understood that that was just
the foundation

00:47:15.256 --> 00:47:22.230
to be heard but that there was a lot more
to be said.

00:47:22.230 --> 00:47:27.500
So for me, owning a network or being a
part of a network is

00:47:27.500 --> 00:47:33.400
about continuing to use that platform to
raise the consciousness.

00:47:33.400 --> 00:47:37.005
I do a show on Sundays, which you can see
live called Super Soul Sunday,

00:47:37.005 --> 00:47:39.110
where I literally talk to thought leaders

00:47:39.110 --> 00:47:41.770
from around the world and ask the
questions.

00:47:41.770 --> 00:47:43.995
Not as good as you, I'm gonna consult with
you.

00:47:43.995 --> 00:47:44.770
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:47:44.770 --> 00:47:47.082
&gt;&gt; Ask the questions in life that really
matter to

00:47:47.082 --> 00:47:50.108
get people thinking about what really
matters in their lives

00:47:50.108 --> 00:47:52.954
and the responses that I get from people,
just regarding

00:47:52.954 --> 00:47:55.467
that show let me know that I'm on the
right track.

00:47:55.467 --> 00:48:00.488
I'm moving in the right direction and so,
I'm not afraid

00:48:00.488 --> 00:48:05.407
because I know that all of us have limited
time here but the

00:48:05.407 --> 00:48:10.651
real question is who are you and what do
you want to do with it?

00:48:10.651 --> 00:48:13.967
And how are you going to use who you are?

00:48:13.967 --> 00:48:20.844
My favorite line from Seed of the Soul is
when the personality, comes

00:48:20.844 --> 00:48:27.500
to serve the energy of your soul, that is
authentic empowerment.

00:48:28.760 --> 00:48:33.801
So as graduates of this great school, to
take what

00:48:33.801 --> 00:48:38.961
you've learned here, to take what is a
part of your

00:48:38.961 --> 00:48:43.762
nature and what you've developed as skills
and

00:48:43.762 --> 00:48:48.922
what really feeds your passion, to take
that and to

00:48:48.922 --> 00:48:54.204
align that with the deeper potential
impossibility

00:48:54.204 --> 00:48:56.750
of your soul's coming.

00:48:57.930 --> 00:49:01.199
If you align your personality with what
your soul

00:49:01.199 --> 00:49:04.227
came to do, and everybody has it, align
your

00:49:04.227 --> 00:49:07.894
personality with your purpose and nobody
can touch you

00:49:07.894 --> 00:49:10.939
and you wake up everyday and you are fired
up.

00:49:10.939 --> 00:49:13.960
You're just like oh, my God, another day!

00:49:13.960 --> 00:49:14.280
It's so great!

00:49:14.280 --> 00:49:15.230
Because everybody has a purpose.

00:49:15.230 --> 00:49:17.780
So you're whole thing is to figure out
what that is.

00:49:17.780 --> 00:49:21.830
Your real job is to figure out why you're
really here and then

00:49:23.880 --> 00:49:25.780
get about the business of doing that.

00:49:25.780 --> 00:49:25.900
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:49:25.900 --> 00:49:26.260
&gt;&gt; That's it.

00:49:26.260 --> 00:49:27.500
&gt;&gt; So we all know now what we have to do,
right?

00:49:27.500 --> 00:49:27.743
Only wait.

00:49:27.743 --> 00:49:28.747
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:49:28.747 --> 00:49:30.500
&gt;&gt; So Oprah, thank you so much.

00:49:30.500 --> 00:49:31.850
&gt;&gt; Are we gonna take some questions?

00:49:31.850 --> 00:49:32.980
&gt;&gt; Well, yeah, so that's what I wanted to
say.

00:49:32.980 --> 00:49:33.490
I'd love to put it up.

00:49:33.490 --> 00:49:34.979
&gt;&gt; Everybody has a class at 1:15, right?

00:49:36.580 --> 00:49:37.635
Okay, I'll get you out of here.

00:49:37.635 --> 00:49:38.380
[Laugh] They told me hard out.

00:49:38.380 --> 00:49:39.080
One o'clock.

00:49:39.080 --> 00:49:39.200
Yeah.

00:49:39.200 --> 00:49:41.347
But so we think we have, do we

00:49:41.347 --> 00:49:45.471
have a first question from Twitter, coming
forward?

00:49:45.471 --> 00:49:51.781
Throughout the session, the first question
asked

00:49:51.781 --> 00:49:58.246
today was Matt Sucedo who asked, will you
marry me?

00:49:58.246 --> 00:49:59.300
[LAUGH].

00:49:59.300 --> 00:50:02.580
&gt;&gt; Oh, it looks like he's up there.

00:50:02.580 --> 00:50:04.220
&gt;&gt; Matt, where's the ring?

00:50:04.220 --> 00:50:04.720
&gt;&gt; Matt, do

00:50:08.070 --> 00:50:08.570
we need marriage?

00:50:12.980 --> 00:50:21.091
Oh my god, that's gonna be such a pre-nup
between us, I've gotta say.

00:50:21.091 --> 00:50:24.420
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What else you got, Andre?

00:50:24.420 --> 00:50:28.047
&gt;&gt; And then we had Javier Hernandez, who
asked,

00:50:28.047 --> 00:50:32.330
Oprah who has been your favorite
interviewee and why?

00:50:32.330 --> 00:50:34.805
&gt;&gt; Well, actually, I would have to say,
there's so

00:50:34.805 --> 00:50:36.878
many over the years and the truth is that
the

00:50:36.878 --> 00:50:39.470
people whose names that I can't even
remember and you

00:50:39.470 --> 00:50:43.640
probably wouldn't remember, have been the
most revelatory, the most impactful.

00:50:43.640 --> 00:50:49.830
I mean, watching people step out of
tragedies

00:50:49.830 --> 00:50:54.760
and define triumph for themselves.

00:50:56.140 --> 00:50:58.750
Those people, really, have been the ones
that really

00:50:58.750 --> 00:51:01.340
shaped me and made me a better human
being.

00:51:01.340 --> 00:51:05.462
I did an interview once with a woman and
actually with Doctor Phil, where

00:51:05.462 --> 00:51:09.722
she had come to the show and then was
planning to kill herself afterwards she

00:51:09.722 --> 00:51:12.224
said because her daughter had been
murdered

00:51:12.224 --> 00:51:14.794
eight years before and she couldn't get
past

00:51:14.794 --> 00:51:18.820
it and she just wanted to come on the
Oprah show and talk about it.

00:51:18.820 --> 00:51:23.932
And Phil said to her, why do you spend all
your time lamenting, all

00:51:23.932 --> 00:51:29.250
these years lamenting the death, instead
of celebrating the life?

00:51:29.250 --> 00:51:31.196
You've let the one day define your

00:51:31.196 --> 00:51:33.681
daughter's entire life and she looked up
at

00:51:33.681 --> 00:51:38.450
him and she said you know, I never thought
about it that way before with tears.

00:51:38.450 --> 00:51:40.810
I could feel that, the shift in her.

00:51:40.810 --> 00:51:43.987
So the most important moments for me have
been

00:51:43.987 --> 00:51:47.497
when literally, I can see that somebody
has made a

00:51:47.497 --> 00:51:50.842
shift in the way they see themselves in
the world

00:51:50.842 --> 00:51:54.124
or you know, what we call now, an aha
moment.

00:51:54.124 --> 00:51:57.401
Those, I live for that, those are my
favorite

00:51:57.401 --> 00:52:02.539
interviews but most recently, I just last
week interviewed Pharrell.

00:52:02.539 --> 00:52:05.125
Oh, my god.

00:52:05.125 --> 00:52:06.062
I was so happy.

00:52:06.062 --> 00:52:09.125
&gt;&gt; But you made him cry.

